body:
Fixes a memory leak in lifeCycleMainService. 





### Details

It seems the way the event listener is setup, when an okChannel event is received, the listener for the cancelChannel is not removed and vice versa. 


```ts
validatedIpcMain.once(okChannel, () => {
  /* 
   * this can run, but if we get into this branch, 
   * we never remove the cancelChannel listener 
   * from below and vice versa
   */
  resolve(false); // no veto 
});

validatedIpcMain.once(cancelChannel, () => {
  resolve(true); // veto
});
```

### Change

The change tries to ensure that always both listeners are removed when either a okChannel event or a cancelChannel event is received.



```ts
const cleanup = (value: boolean) => {
  /* Always ensure to clean up both listeners */
  validatedIpcMain.removeListener(okChannel, okListener);
  validatedIpcMain.removeListener(cancelChannel, cancelListener);
  resolve(value);
};

const okListener = () => {
  cleanup(false); // no veto
};

const cancelListener = () => {
  cleanup(true); // veto
};

validatedIpcMain.on(okChannel, okListener);
validatedIpcMain.on(cancelChannel, cancelListener);
```


### Before


When opening and closing a new window 17 times, the number of `CodeWindow` instances and some other functions seems to grow by 1 each time:

<img width="1400" height="400" alt="codewindow" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/be4ac74d-7022-401c-89b5-cd3231ac18de" />


### After

When opening and closing a new window 17 times, no more `CodeWindow` leak is detected. And overall less functions seem to be leaked:

<img width="1400" height="240" alt="window-open-new-code-window" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/887a7e5c-bc64-4a95-8720-abc333f6523e" />

Closing the integrated terminal while a background `run_in_terminal` monitor is active can produce a spurious `<cmd> may need input` steering turn that consumes Copilot usage, even though the user did not submit a prompt and there is no live terminal to send input to.

## Root cause

`RunInTerminalTool._registerCompletionNotification` subscribes to `outputMonitor.onDidDetectInputNeeded` for background terminals. When the user closes the terminal, the monitor's idle/dispose race can still fire that event because the buffered scrollback ends in a line matching an input-required pattern (e.g. a pager-prompt tail). The listener then queues a steering `sendRequest` even though the terminal is gone.

## Changes

- **`runInTerminalTool.ts`** — early-return in the `onDidDetectInputNeeded` handler when `terminalInstance.isDisposed` is `true`. The existing `terminalInstance.onDisposed` handler still posts the separate "terminal exited" steering message, so the agent is not left unaware of the lifecycle change — only the redundant input-needed turn is suppressed.
- **`runInTerminalTool.test.ts`** — regression test that fires `inputNeeded` after marking the terminal disposed and asserts no steering chat request is captured.

```ts
store.add(outputMonitor.onDidDetectInputNeeded(() => {
    if (userIsReplyingDirectly) { /* ... */ return; }

    // Terminal closed by the user — buffered output may still look like an
    // input prompt, but there is nowhere to send input. The onDisposed
    // listener below handles notifying the agent that the terminal exited.
    if (terminalInstance.isDisposed) {
        return;
    }

    if (handleSessionCancelled()) { return; }
    // ...
}));
```
